**task_02.md** content:

Title: #921 Gap 2 — a relaxed comparison whose assertion message carries a digit scores UNKNOWN; scope the integer scan to the condition, not the message
Kind: evolve
Files: scripts/check_assertion_weakening.py
Issue: #921

Description:
`_relaxed_comparison` (line ~625) splits the removed/added assertion text on the comparison operator and requires exactly ONE integer literal on the right-hand side. Its docstring (line ~555) states that requirement. When the assertion's human-readable MESSAGE also carries a number — `assert!(count >= 2, "flag '{long}' is less than 2 characters")` — the right-hand side carries two integer literals, the guard bails, and the shape returns `None`, so a genuine relaxation scores UNKNOWN instead of WEAKENED. Measured by the Day-198 positive control on a scratch ripgrep clone (plant P4 scored UNKNOWN); it bounds my own readings too, because the digit is in the message and the message is not part of the condition.

The fix: make the integer-literal boundary end where the CONDITION ends — the MESSAGE is not part of it. The function already knows where the condition ends (the same line ~542 docstring names "the CONDITION portion of an assert-macro line. The MESSAGE is not part of it"). Scope the RHS integer extraction to the condition text, so a digit inside the message can no longer affect the count. Do not widen anything else: `ASSERT_MACRO_RE`, `TEST_ATTR_RE`, the operator set, and the "exactly one integer literal" rule itself stay as they are. The defect is WHERE the count is taken, not how many literals it requires.

Required guards (both directions, or the fix is this defect wearing the opposite sign):
1. It FIRES: a self-test whose removed line is the digit-bearing-message shape above and whose added line is a genuine relaxation of the same comparison → the pair classifies WEAKENED. Assembly detail to make it non-vacuous: run the SAME fixture through the pre-fix behaviour in the control step below and watch it score UNKNOWN, so the case is pinned as a transition rather than as agreement.
2. It does NOT fire on a near miss: an assertion whose condition itself carries two integer literals on the RHS (e.g. a range check `count >= 2 && count <= 10`) must still return None — the one-literal requirement is what keeps the shape honest, and a matcher that fires on everything is quieter than the bug.
